course: dictionary
lesson: 11101
title: Old words: whence
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: «Whence did he come?» sounds old, but the context helps. Come is a word about travelling, so the question must be asking where he started from. Whence means from where. In modern speech we say «Where did he come from?»
explain: «Whence» is an old-fashioned, formal adverb. You mostly meet it in old stories, poems, fairy tales and the Bible, where it gives a grand feel. People today say «where … from» instead.
explain: Use the swap check. In «the town whence she came», put in «the town from which she came» and the sentence still works, so whence means from which place. In «He was late, whence the trouble», the trouble comes from being late, so whence means which is why.
fact: Whence is an adverb, and it is old-fashioned and formal.
fact: «Whence» means from where: «Whence did he come?» means «Where did he come from?»
fact: «Whence» can also mean from which place: «the town whence she came».
fact: «Whence» can mean which is why: «He was late, whence the trouble.»
fact: Related old words: «hence» means from here, «thence» means from there, and «whither» means to where.
exercise: «Whence did he come?» What does whence mean here? | Very quickly | To where | Why he was tired | *From where
exercise: How would you say «whence» in modern English in «Whence did she come?» | Why did she come? | Where did she go? | When did she arrive? | *Where did she come from?
exercise: Which old word means from there? | hence | trudging | *thence | whither
test: In «the town whence she came», whence means | in the morning | very loudly | a kind of food | *from which place
test: In «He was late, whence the trouble», whence means | *which is why | very quickly | a new shoe | in the morning
exam: The Bible verse «from whence cometh my help» means | why I need help | *from where comes my help | to where my help goes | help is near
